<!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<section class="cs-exec">
  <div class="cs-exec-inner">
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Managing a seven-campaign Google Ads account at more than $1M in annual spend is a different exercise from managing two campaigns at a fraction of that. Not because the principles change, but because the financial consequence of each structural decision is proportionally larger. A targeting inefficiency that costs a smaller account a few hundred dollars a month costs a large account several thousand.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Home and lifestyle brands at this scale typically carry catalogs with meaningful variation: different product categories, different average order values, different purchase cycles, different search volumes. Seven campaigns serving that range are not automatically seven campaigns with clearly defined and complementary roles. At some point, a portfolio requires deliberate management rather than incremental adjustment.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">At more than $1M in Google Ads spend, a 4× blended ROAS is already a meaningful result. The question was not whether the account had one strong campaign. It was whether all seven campaigns were contributing appropriately to the broader commercial objective, and whether the account was being managed as a portfolio or as seven isolated lines in a report.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Our review focused on how campaign roles, Search coverage, product data, bidding targets, and budget allocation were working together across the account. We found several areas where the account structure had not been revisited in light of the account's current scale and composition.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">A portfolio at this spend level that hasn't been formally reviewed in a while tends to develop exactly these patterns. Not because anything went wrong — because nobody went back to check whether the original decisions still fit the account's current scale.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">During the documented January–May 2026 period, the seven active campaigns generated $4.33M in attributed revenue from $1.07M in managed spend, a 4.06× blended ROAS. Reported campaign-level ROAS ranged from 3.75× to 5.02×, with different campaigns serving different forms of demand.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FINDINGS -->
<section class="cs-findings">
  <div class="cs-findings-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">What the Account Review Revealed</h2>

    <div class="cs-findings-list" role="list">
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">All seven campaigns shared one efficiency target</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">All seven campaigns were using the same target ROAS setting, despite serving different types of demand. A Brand Search campaign capturing high-intent branded queries, a feed-driven Shopping campaign, and a broader acquisition campaign may each contribute differently to the account's commercial objective. Applying one identical efficiency expectation across all three can obscure those differences and constrain delivery in ways that do not reflect each campaign's role.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Campaign responsibilities were not clearly defined</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Multiple campaigns covered closely related Search demand, reducing visibility into which campaign was intended to serve each query type. The issue was not internal auction competition; it was governance. Query routing, budget interpretation, and campaign-level performance were harder to evaluate when campaign responsibilities overlapped.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Product titles were written at the category level, not the product level</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Several feed titles relied on broad category language or internal catalog terminology rather than the specific product attributes customers used in Search. The campaigns were structured around high-intent product queries, but the feed titles did not consistently reflect that language at the individual SKU level.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">04</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Some bidding targets reflected an earlier version of the account</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Certain targets had remained unchanged while the account's campaign structure, conversion behavior, and product mix had evolved. Targets that no longer reflect current account conditions can constrain delivery or permit lower-efficiency volume, depending on how they are set relative to achievable performance. At seven campaigns and $1M+ in spend, those effects compound.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-mid-hook">
      <p class="cs-mid-hook-text">At $1M+ in spend, account structures that worked at a smaller scale may no longer reflect the brand's current product mix, campaign economics, or growth objectives. Even modest inefficiencies across a portfolio of seven campaigns carry a meaningful dollar consequence.</p>
      <a href="../growth-diagnostic-landing.html#book" class="cs-mid-hook-link">The Growth Diagnostic examines whether the system behind your current performance still fits the scale of the account &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

