<!-- WHAT WE CHANGED -->
<section class="cs-interventions">
  <div class="cs-interventions-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">What We Changed Across the Account</h2>

    <div class="cs-intervention-list">
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Aligned bidding targets with campaign role</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We reviewed the seven campaigns against their current performance, demand type, and commercial purpose. Where the evidence supported it, target ROAS settings were adjusted so campaigns were not all being managed against one identical efficiency expectation. A Brand Search campaign and a broader acquisition campaign serve different forms of demand and may not warrant the same target.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Created clearer query responsibilities across the portfolio</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We reviewed where Search, Shopping, and Performance Max campaigns covered related demand and applied available negative-keyword, exclusion, and campaign-setting controls to improve query governance. The goal was not perfect separation. It was clearer accountability for which campaign was responsible for each type of traffic.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Rebuilt product feed titles at the SKU level</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">Product titles were rewritten around the attributes customers were more likely to use in Search rather than internal catalog terminology. Where appropriate, titles front-loaded product type, material, color, size, and style. Visibility on targeted high-intent product searches improved after the revised titles were approved.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Reset bidding targets against current account conditions</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">Certain targets reflected an earlier version of the account. We reviewed them against current conversion performance, product economics, campaign role, and delivery constraints, then updated them where the evidence supported a different balance between efficiency and volume. Targets that no longer fit current conditions can constrain delivery or permit lower-efficiency volume. Across seven campaigns, that compounds.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Reviewed budget allocation across the portfolio</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">Budget decisions were reviewed against campaign role, conversion value, efficiency, available demand, and the account's broader commercial priorities. Allocation was adjusted where the evidence indicated a different distribution would better reflect each campaign's contribution to the account's overall objective.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-callout">
      <p class="cs-callout-text">At $1M+ in spend, one high-performing campaign is not enough to explain account health. The more important question is whether the broader portfolio is contributing appropriately across brand demand, category demand, product coverage, seasonal activity, and new-customer acquisition. During the documented period, all seven campaigns recorded ROAS between 3.75× and 5.02×. Those figures reflect different campaign roles within a 4.06× blended account result.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- PERFORMANCE SUMMARY -->
<section class="scale-outcome" aria-label="Portfolio outcome">
  <div class="scale-outcome-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2" id="outcome-heading">Portfolio Outcome</h2>
    <div class="scale-outcome-content">
      <p class="cs-para">Across the documented January–May 2026 period, seven campaigns generated $4.33M in attributed revenue from $1.07M in managed spend, a 4.06&times; blended ROAS. Campaign-level reported ROAS ranged from 3.75&times; to 5.02&times;, consistent with the different strategic roles each campaign served within the account.</p>
      <p class="cs-para">The significance of this result is not the headline number. It is that all seven campaigns could be evaluated against their own contribution rather than a single efficiency expectation, and the portfolio as a whole could be managed with clearer accountability at the campaign level.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="scale-rec-note" role="note" aria-label="Platform recommendation note">
      <span class="scale-rec-eyebrow">Platform-Generated Recommendation</span>
      <p class="scale-rec-body">During the documented period, Google Ads displayed an "Optimize your budgets" recommendation. This indicates that Google's forecasting system identified potential additional conversion value from a different budget allocation or higher total budget. Platform recommendations are forecasts based on Google's models, not guarantees of future revenue or maintained ROAS. Any expansion would need to be evaluated against marginal efficiency, profitability, available inventory, and the brand's growth objectives.</p>
    </div>

    <span class="scale-attribution">All performance data sourced directly from the client's Google Ads account. Platform-attributed revenue. January 1 – May 31, 2026.</span>
  </div>
</section>

